    {
      "crop_slug": "bee-balm",
      "field_path": "companions.note_seasoned",
      "our_prose": "Bee balm's companion role is the pollinator-magnet inversion: it is the plant you site to benefit others. As one of the premier native nectar sources for bumblebees, long-tongued bees, butterflies, and ruby-throated hummingbirds, it is interplanted near insect-pollinated crops and as an insectary border to lift pollinator and beneficial-predator visitation. There is no biologically antagonistic companion to avoid; the real cautions are horticultural. It spreads aggressively by rhizomes, so contain it or divide it to keep it off slower neighbors, and do not crowd it against dense, disease-prone plants, because trapped humidity raises shared powdery-mildew pressure. Site for airflow.",
      "run_normalized": "butterflies and ruby throated hummingbirds it is",
